"Hi, my name is Frank Nkurunziza, and this is ALU InternHub — a mobile application I built using Flutter and Firebase that connects ALU students with student-led startups for internship opportunities.

The problem I'm solving is simple: ALU students need internship experience, and ALU startups need talent — but there's no dedicated platform connecting them. ALU InternHub bridges that gap.

In this demo I'll walk you through the authentication, Firebase integration, state management, and all core features."

PART 1 — AUTHENTICATION (0:30 – 1:30)
Show the onboarding screen

"When a user opens the app, they land on the onboarding screen where they choose their role — either Student or Startup. This role-based design is intentional. Students and startups have completely different needs, so they get completely different experiences."

Register as a Startup

"Let me first register as a startup. I'll use an ALU email address — notice that if I try a non-ALU email, the app rejects it with a validation error. This enforces that only ALU community members can join the platform."

Type a non-ALU email → show error → then use a real ALU email

"Now let me switch to my Firebase Console — you can see this new user appearing in the Authentication tab in real time."

Show Firebase Console → Authentication → Users

"And in Firestore, under the users collection, the user document was automatically created with their role, email, and full name."

Show Firestore → users collection → new document

PART 2 — STARTUP ONBOARDING & VERIFICATION (1:30 – 2:30)
Show CreateStartup screen

"After a startup registers, they're taken directly to the startup creation screen. They fill in their startup name, description, industry, stage, and team size."

"The key feature here is the ALU Verification Code field. This is how we ensure only legitimate ALU-registered startups can post opportunities. When they submit, their startup is created with a verification status of pending."

Fill in the form and submit

"Let me show you Firestore — the startup document is now created with verificationStatus set to pending. Until an admin approves it, this startup cannot post visible opportunities."

Show Firestore → startups collection → new document → verificationStatus: pending

"For this demo, I'll manually change the status to approved in Firestore to simulate the admin verification."

Change verificationStatus to approved in Firestore Console

"Notice how the app updates in real time — the pending warning banner disappears because we're using Firestore streams, not one-time fetches."

PART 3 — POSTING AN OPPORTUNITY (2:30 – 3:30)
Show Post Opportunity screen

"Now as an approved startup, I can post an opportunity. I'll fill in the role title, category, description, duration, whether it's remote and paid, required skills, number of spots, and the application deadline."

Fill in the form quickly

"When I tap Post Opportunity, watch the Firestore Console."

Submit and immediately show Firestore

"The opportunity document appears instantly in the opportunities collection — this is a real-time write to Firestore. The opportunity is now live and visible to all students on the platform."

PART 4 — STUDENT DISCOVERY & APPLICATION (3:30 – 5:00)
Switch to student account

"Now let me switch to a student account. I'll sign out and log in as a student."

Sign out → login as student

"On the Discover tab, students see all active opportunities in real time. I can search by keyword, filter by role type like Software Development or Design, and toggle remote-only opportunities."

Demonstrate search and filters

"This filtering happens locally in the OpportunityProvider using a filtered list method — so it's instant with no extra Firestore reads."

Open opportunity detail

"Let me tap on the opportunity we just posted. I can see all the details — the startup, role type, duration, stipend, required skills, and deadline."

"I'll tap Apply Now. A bottom sheet slides up where I write my cover letter."

Write cover letter and submit

"The application is submitted. Watch Firestore — the application document appears immediately in the applications collection with status set to pending."

Show Firestore → applications collection

"The Apply button has also changed to Application Submitted — this is Provider state management in action. The AuthProvider checked hasApplied, updated the state, and the UI rebuilt automatically."

Show My Applications tab

"In the Applied tab, the student can track all their applications and see their current status in real time."

PART 5 — STARTUP MANAGES APPLICATIONS (5:00 – 5:45)
Switch back to startup account

"Now let me switch back to the startup account. In the Applications tab, I can see the application Frank just submitted."

"I can see the applicant's name, email, the opportunity they applied for, and their cover letter. I have two actions — Accept or Reject."

Tap Accept

"I'll accept this application. Watch Firestore — the status field updates from pending to accepted instantly."

Show Firestore → applications → status: accepted

"And if the student refreshes their applications screen, they'll see the status has changed to Accepted — this is real-time synchronization between two different user accounts through Firestore streams."

PART 6 — STATE MANAGEMENT EXPLANATION (5:45 – 6:30)
"Let me quickly explain how state management works in this app. I used the Provider package with ChangeNotifier."

"There are four providers — AuthProvider, StartupProvider, OpportunityProvider, and ApplicationProvider. They're all registered at the root of the app in main.dart using MultiProvider."

"When something changes — for example when a user bookmarks an opportunity — the AuthProvider calls notifyListeners(). Every widget that's listening with context.watch rebuilds automatically. Widgets that just need to call a method use context.read, which doesn't trigger rebuilds."

"This gives clean separation between UI and business logic. The screens don't talk to Firebase directly — they go through the providers, which handle all Firestore operations."

OUTRO (6:30 – 7:00)
"To summarize — ALU InternHub solves a real problem at ALU. It uses Firebase Authentication with ALU email enforcement, Cloud Firestore for real-time CRUD operations, Provider for state management, and GoRouter for role-based navigation."

"The architecture is feature-based, meaning each feature — auth, startups, opportunities, applications, profile — is fully self-contained and independently scalable."

"Future improvements I would add include push notifications, resume uploads, an admin verification dashboard, and a skill-based recommendation system."

"Thank you."

